.Dd November 4, 2026
.Dt DMESG 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm dmesg
.Nd read retained boot and runtime kernel diagnostics
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl w
.Op Fl -level Ar level
.Op Fl -source Ar source
.Op Fl -since Ar sequence
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
prints a nondestructive snapshot of the kernel diagnostic ring. With
.Fl w
or
.Fl -follow
it also polls for later records. Every reader has an independent cursor.
.Fl -since
starts at the specified boot-local sequence; zero starts at retained oldest.
.Fl -source
matches the exact source token.
.Fl -level
includes the named level and all more severe levels: emerg, alert, crit,
err, warn, notice, info, debug. The default includes every level.
.Pp
Each line names capture sequence, monotonic timer ticks when available
.Pq otherwise ? ,
severity and source. Early streamed console diagnostics infer source from
an initial token and severity from PANIC, FAILED, ERROR or WARN text;
structured producers provide explicit metadata. Inferred records carry
inferred; fragments affected by producer contention carry damaged, with
unknown source and a conservative informational level. Severity and source
filters on inferred records compare those disclosed estimates. Damaged records
remain visible through severity filters because their original level may be
lost; an explicit source filter still applies. Capture order does not
establish cross-CPU causality. The header names the boot identity, or
unknown if the kernel cannot supply one. No wall-clock date is invented.
.Pp
Retention is 128 records of at most 480 message bytes. Long lines retain
their prefix and carry truncated. Fatal non-newline fragments carry partial.
Overwritten sequences and producer contention drops are explicit. A snapshot
ends at the sequence boundary observed by its first read; a busy producer
may overwrite history while it is read. No read clears the ring.
.Pp
All terminal controls, newlines inside messages and non-ASCII bytes are
printed as hexadecimal escapes; literal backslashes are doubled. Filters
operate on original metadata. This is safe display text, not a reversible
binary export format.
.Pp
Reading uses the explicit inherited read-only Diagnostics capability through
branch-local experimental syscall 0x7fc0. Init mints this grant using 0x7fc3
and delegates it as startup role20; runtime children inherit a narrowed read
copy even when stdout is a pipe. The grant confers no terminal input, physical
display write or machine-control rights. Only root init can fall back to its
private writable Console. A missing, closed, wrong-kind or insufficient-right
handle is refused. Handle zero resolves existing authority and creates none.
There is no clear operation. Kernel diagnostics may include device and kernel
addresses already emitted by the existing console. This operator image delegates
the grant throughout the runtime child tree, including children without file
access; there is currently no per-spawn diagnostic exclusion. Do not use this
profile for applications that must be excluded from kernel diagnostics, or log
credentials.
.Pp
The command reads the kernel directly and does not contact the file server
or network. Its ordinary shell and executable loading still require their
normal services. The companion local console's kernel maintenance view supplies the independent
emergency access route when composed into the image.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 on snapshot completion or output closure; 1 on kernel refusal;
2 on invalid options. Follow continues until interrupted or output closes.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr afterboot 8 ,
.Xr uptime 1
